# <Project name>

<One or two sentences: what the C++ code does, and what the Python tools analyse.>
Example: High-performance simulation engine in C++20. Python tools in `analysis/` post-process its output files and produce plots and reports.

## Layout
- `src/`, `include/` - C++ library and executables
- `tests/` - C++ unit tests (<GoogleTest | Catch2>)
- `analysis/` - Python analysis tools (package: `<pkgname>`)
- `analysis/tests/` - pytest suite
- `cmake/`, `CMakePresets.json` - build configuration
- `third_party/` - vendored code. **Do not edit.**
- `build/` - out-of-source build dirs. **Never commit, never edit by hand.**

## Commands

### C++
- Configure: `cmake --preset dev`
- Build: `cmake --build --preset dev -j`
- Test all: `ctest --preset dev --output-on-failure`
- Test one: `ctest --preset dev -R <TestName>`
- Sanitizer build: `cmake --preset asan && cmake --build --preset asan`
- Format: `clang-format -i <files>` (config in `.clang-format`)
- Lint: `clang-tidy -p build/dev <file>`

### Python (run from `analysis/`)
- Setup: `uv sync` (or `python -m venv .venv && pip install -e ".[dev]"`)
- Test: `uv run pytest -q`
- Lint and format: `uv run ruff check --fix . && uv run ruff format .`
- Types: `uv run mypy <pkgname>`

Before saying a task is done, build and run the tests for whichever side you changed.

## C++ conventions
- Standard: C++<20>. Do not use compiler-specific extensions.
- Manage ownership with RAII. Use `std::unique_ptr` by default and `std::shared_ptr` only when ownership is genuinely shared. No raw `new`/`delete`.
- Pass by `const&` or `std::span`/`std::string_view` for read-only data.
- Errors: <exceptions | `std::expected` | error codes>. Follow the existing pattern in the module you are editing.
- Headers use `#pragma once`. Include what you use. Don't add `using namespace` in headers.
- Naming: <`PascalCase` types, `snake_case` functions/variables, `kConstant`, `member_` suffix>
- New code needs a unit test in `tests/` that mirrors the source path.
- Performance-sensitive code is in `<src/core/>`. Avoid allocations in hot loops, and don't change algorithms there without saying so.

## Python conventions
- Python <3.11+>. Type hints on all public functions.
- Use numpy/pandas vectorised operations rather than Python loops over large data.
- Tools are CLI entry points (`<argparse | click | typer>`). Keep the logic in importable functions so tests can call it without the CLI.
- Never hardcode paths to data. Take them as arguments.
- Plots: <matplotlib>. Save to files, never call `plt.show()` in tools.

## C++ / Python boundary
- The Python tools read C++ output in <format: CSV | HDF5 | JSON | binary layout>. The schema is defined in `<include/.../output_format.hpp>` and mirrored in `<analysis/pkgname/io.py>`.
- **If you change the output format, update both sides and the tests on both sides in the same change.**
- <If using pybind11/nanobind: bindings live in `src/bindings/`. Rebuild with `cmake --build --preset dev --target <module>` before running Python tests that import it.>

## Don't
- Edit `third_party/`, generated files, or anything under `build/`.
- Change `CMakePresets.json`, CI config, or compiler flags unless asked.
- Add new dependencies (C++ or Python) without asking first.
- Commit large data files. Test fixtures go in `<tests/data/>` and stay under <1 MB>.

## Gotchas
<Add things that trip people up. Examples:>
- <Full build takes ~N min. Build only the target you need: `cmake --build --preset dev --target <name>`.>
- <Tests in `tests/integration/` need `DATA_DIR` set and are slow. Skip them unless you touched I/O.>
